          <span class="faq-icon"><i class="ti ti-plus"></i></span>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-answer"><p>It is a learning management system built into the platform: role-based learning paths, a course library and course builder, sort, filter, and search across content, role-based access and visibility, certifications by product or category, and a manager dashboard for HQ. What makes it different from a standalone LMS is that course material synchronizes from live product records, so training reflects the current assortment without anyone rebuilding it.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-question" onclick="this.parentElement.classList.toggle('open')">
          How does associate training work?
          <span class="faq-icon"><i class="ti ti-plus"></i></span>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-answer"><p>Training is built into the platform rather than bolted on: it delivers role-based learning, AI-powered coaching, and product education using live product information from your catalog, so associates always train on the current assortment.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-question" onclick="this.parentElement.classList.toggle('open')">
          How is training assigned to associates?
          <span class="faq-icon"><i class="ti ti-plus"></i></span>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-answer"><p>Learning paths assign role-based modules automatically, based on each associate's role. When a team member is onboarded or changes role, their training assignments follow without manual administration.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-question" onclick="this.parentElement.classList.toggle('open')">
          How does training stay current with the product catalog?
          <span class="faq-icon"><i class="ti ti-plus"></i></span>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-answer"><p>Catalog synchronization pulls training material directly from live product records. As products and enrichments change, the training content associates see reflects the latest product information rather than a static course written earlier in the season.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-question" onclick="this.parentElement.classList.toggle('open')">
          What are AI simulations and certifications?
          <span class="faq-icon"><i class="ti ti-plus"></i></span>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-answer"><p>AI simulations are voice and text role-play scenarios that mimic customer interactions and evaluate an associate's product knowledge. Certifications track progress and award certified status for a product or category, so managers can see who is cleared to sell what. AI simulations use the same product records as the rest of the platform.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-question" onclick="this.parentElement.classList.toggle('open')">
          Who can see which courses, and how do managers track progress?
          <span class="faq-icon"><i class="ti ti-plus"></i></span>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-answer"><p>Access and visibility in the course library are role-based, so associates only see the courses that apply to their role. Course content can be sorted, filtered, and searched to reach the right material quickly, and a manager dashboard gives HQ employees a view of progress and certification status across the team.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-question" onclick="this.parentElement.classList.toggle('open')">
          Where do associates take the training?
          <span class="faq-icon"><i class="ti ti-plus"></i></span>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-answer"><p>Training is delivered through the mobile associate app they already use for product search, customer recommendations, and POS login. It runs on personal or company phones on iOS and Android, so there is no separate learning portal to deploy.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

